            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>The Scene: Monday Morning, the Campaign Manager Is Stressed</h2>
<p>Monday morning. Xiao Lin, campaign manager at a home goods cross-border e-commerce team, stared at the spreadsheet in silence.</p>
<p>Three team members managing ten ad accounts—2 Meta, 3 Google, 2 TikTok, and 3 testing Snapchat. The problem: all ten accounts had different spend velocities, campaign stages, and health statuses. Someone would wake up to find an account had run dry overnight. Someone else discovered an account had been disabled but nobody knew.</p>
<p>This wasn't a budget issue. It was a management issue.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Problem Diagnosis: Three Typical Multi-Account Chaos Patterns</h2>
<p>After analysis, Xiao Lin's team identified three root causes:</p>
<p><strong>1. "One-size-fits-all" Budget Allocation</strong></p>
<p>All accounts received uniform fixed budgets, yet some accounts consistently showed above-average ROI while others were still in testing phase. "No idea which ones deserved more spend or which needed cutting back. Everything was gut feeling."</p>
<p><strong>2. Manual Status Monitoring</strong></p>
<p>Tracking balance, delivery status, and policy violations across ten accounts relied entirely on manual checks. "One person can't keep up. Usually we'd only find out an account was disabled after customer complaints came in."</p>
<p><strong>3. Undifferentiated Access Permissions</strong></p>
<p>Three team members with varying skill levels all had access to all accounts. "A new intern almost shut down the main performing ad group."</p>
<hr />
<h2>Judgment: Account Tiering Is Based on Value, Not Quantity</h2>
<p>Xiao Lin decided to implement account tiering. The first step was establishing tiering criteria, not mechanically splitting accounts by number.</p>
<p>The team defined three dimensions:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Dimension</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>ROI Stability</strong></td>
<td>CPA fluctuation over past 30 days exceeding 20%?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Spend Scale</strong></td>
<td>Daily spend as percentage of total budget</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Account Health</strong></td>
<td>Violation history, payment status</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>After scoring, ten accounts fell into three categories:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tier A (Main Accounts)</strong>: 3 accounts, contributing 60% of spend, stable ROI</li>
<li><strong>Tier B (Potential Accounts)</strong>: 4 accounts, data accumulated but volatile ROI</li>
<li><strong>Tier C (Test Accounts)</strong>: 3 accounts, newly opened or under adjustment</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Actions: From Tiering to Execution Loop</h2>
<h3>Action 1: Differentiated Budget Allocation</h3>
<p>Tier A accounts use a "stable + flexible" model: 80% fixed base budget, remaining 20% dynamically adjusted based on daily performance.</p>
<p>Tier B accounts use an "observe + accelerate" model: daily data review, budget increased 20% if ROI targets hit for 3 consecutive days, or paused otherwise.</p>
<p>Tier C accounts use a "small steps, fast iterations" model: daily budget cap set at 50% of Tier B, no additional budget until 100 conversion events achieved.</p>
<h3>Action 2: Tiered Balance Alert Thresholds</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Account Tier</th>
<th>Alert Threshold</th>
<th>Response Deadline</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Tier A</td>
<td>Balance below 1.5x daily spend</td>
<td>Within 2 hours</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Tier B</td>
<td>Balance below 2x daily spend</td>
<td>By end of workday</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Tier C</td>
<td>Balance below 3x daily spend</td>
<td>Next morning meeting decision</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Action 3: Permission Matrix Isolation</h3>
<p>Interns can only operate Tier C accounts; junior operators can manage Tier B; Tier A accounts are restricted to Xiao Lin and senior operators. All permission changes are logged for audit trails.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Results: Changes After Three Months</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Before</th>
<th>After</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Average time to detect account issues</td>
<td>12 hours</td>
<td>2 hours</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Tier A account spend proportion</td>
<td>Variable</td>
<td>Stable at 58-62%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Account disablement losses</td>
<td>2/month</td>
<td>0/month</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Team daily time on account operations</td>
<td>4 hours</td>
<td>1.5 hours</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Xiao Lin noted the most significant change wasn't the metrics—it's that the team stopped feeling anxious. "Before, ten accounts felt like ten ticking time bombs. Now each account knows its role, and we know what to watch."</p>
<hr />
<h2>One Key Insight</h2>
<p>The essence of multi-account management isn't "managing accounts"—it's <strong>managing attention</strong>. The core value of tiering is directing limited attention resources toward the highest-value accounts.</p>
<p>If you're facing similar challenges, start today: score your accounts first, then decide how much time and budget each deserves.</p>
